{"step_index":161,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:39:20Z","thinking":"**Investigating the n=8 Case**\n\nExamining the case where n=8, the maximum possible radius is 4.  Considering if radius can equal 4 when the remote distance (rd) is also 4 requires further investigation. The assumption is if rd=4, the vertex set is defined by the closed neighborhoods of two centers; a previously proven theorem suggests that such graphs have radius at most 3. Need to re-examine that theorem's validity.\n\n\n**Revisiting Radius & Remote Distance**\n\nAnalyzing graphs where the vertex set is the union of two closed neighborhoods, the radius is always at most 3, regardless of the number of vertices.  This means if the remote distance is at most 4, the radius plus one is also at most the remote distance. However, if the remote distance is 5, a radius of 5 requires diameter at least 9, implying at least 10 vertices.  Now considering ways to construct a graph with rd=5.\n\n\n**Formalizing the Theorem in Lean**\n\nIt is clear that for remote distance of 5, the radius is at most 4. This implies radius + 1 is at most 5 and at most the remote distance. It is true for all graphs, not just this specific one. To formalize the proof, attempting to prove `radius_plus_one_le_romanDominationNumber` in Lean 4 seems like the correct approach.\n\nConsidering the existing lemmas, the goal is to prove `k ≤ GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber G`, where k equals GraphCalc.radius G + 1. The proof involves applying relevant lemmas to establish the bound.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c '\nimport glob\nfor p in glob.glob(\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/lean_project/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Combinatorics/SimpleGraph/**/*.lean\", recursive=True):\n    with open(p) as f:\n        txt = f.read()\n    if \"card\" in txt and \"length\" in txt:\n        for line in txt.splitlines():\n            if \"length\" in line and \"card\" in line and (\"theorem\" in line or \"lemma\" in line):\n                print(line)\n'","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Searching Mathlib for path length bound by card","toolSummary":"Search Mathlib path length bounds"}}]}
